  "slug": "building-compartments-670d",
  "summary": "Recent preprints couple phenotypic switching, adhesion and drug-driven dynamics to spatial organization in tumour and tissue models.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of explicit dynamical models to relate cell-level behaviour to emergent spatial organization in growing or invading populations. A Bayesian-fitted ODE coagulation-fragmentation-proliferation model contrasts proliferative and invasive cluster dynamics in melanoma monoculture and co-culture. An adhesion-driven framework links adhesion-mediated invasion to pattern formation, with adhesion strength and its density dependence as key parameters. A go-or-grow model with ECM-dependent phenotypic switching yields an analytical traveling wave speed independent of matrix. A multi-patch ODE model of transgenic oncolytic virotherapy uses spectral reduction to identify R0 regimes and drug-driven Hopf oscillations. The tumour containment study frames malignant connectivity as an anti-percolation process. The items are heterogeneous in scope, but the novelty lies in the joint treatment of phenotypic plasticity, adhesion and drug effects as drivers of spatial organization within a single dynamical framing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-30"
}
